        <body>
            <div xml:id="d209">

                <pb facs="0361" n="267" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg267"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/471: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Abbott</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-03-08T11:00:00+02:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 8,
                                1924—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 7:57
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>43. This morning I received reliable information that as the Cafandaris cabinet
                    is unable to maintain program because of strong republican pressure it is about
                    to resign.</p>
                <p>The republicans are demanding of the Assembly a declaration that the Glücksburg
                    dynasty is abolished and a pronouncement in favor of a republic, the latter
                    question, however, to be referred to a plebiscite which from present indications
                    will be held in May.</p>
                <p>Up to now the political situation has been completely indeterminate. A crisis is
                    at hand, however, and it is not at all improbable <pb facs="0362" n="268"
                        xml:id="pg268"/> that there will be a <hi rendition="#italic">coup
                        d’état</hi> by the republican army group under General [Condylis?] unless an
                    agreement is reached within a few days.</p>
                <p>Sir Milne Cheetham, the British Minister, arrived at Athens February 26 but did
                    not present his letters of credence until today at noon. The other countries
                    represented here by Minister are Bulgaria, Jugo-Slavia and Spain. I am informed
                    by the Italian Chargé that for all practical purposes his relations with the
                    cabinets of Venizelos and Cafandaris have been such as to constitute recognition
                    of the Greek Government. I learn from French representative that attitude of
                    France has not changed and that the Greek Government has not received French
                    recognition.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Abbott</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
